🕸️ Burp Suite
🎯 Purpose
Burp Suite is an integrated platform and industry-standard web application security testing tool. It functions as an intercepting proxy, allowing security analysts to inspect, modify, and analyze web traffic (HTTP/HTTPS) between a client browser and target web servers.
🧠 Key Concepts
Intercepting Proxy: Captures HTTP/HTTPS traffic passing between the browser and the web server, enabling real-time inspection and request modification.
Target & Scope: Defines which hostnames, IP addresses, or URLs belong to the authorized target to prevent accidental testing out of scope.
Repeater: A tool for manually modifying and resending individual HTTP requests to observe server responses under varying inputs.
Intruder: An automated tool used to perform customized, repetitive attacks against web applications (e.g., parameter fuzzing, credential stuffing, brute-forcing).
Decoder: A utility for quickly transforming raw data using common web encoding/decoding schemes (e.g., URL encoding, Base64, Hex, HTML entity).
Comparer: A visual diffing tool to compare two requests or responses to spot subtle structural or data differences.
💻 Commands & Workflows
Burp Suite operates primarily through its Graphical User Interface (GUI), but relies on configuring system/browser proxy settings:
```
# Default Local Proxy Listener Configuration
Host: 127.0.0.1
Port: 8080

# Importing Burp's CA Certificate into Browser
1. Access http://burp in a browser configured with Burp's proxy
2. Download 'cacert.der'
3. Import into browser certificate store as a Trusted Root CA
```
Key Intruder Payload Positions Syntax:
```http
POST /login HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

username=§admin§&password=§password123§
```
📋 Examples
Intercepting and Modifying Request Parameters:
Turn Intercept On under the Proxy tab.
Trigger a web request in the browser (e.g., updating user profile details).
Modify parameters directly in the proxy view (e.g., changing `role=user` to `role=admin`).
Click Forward to send the tampered request to the server.
Fuzzing Parameters using Repeater:
Right-click any captured request $\rightarrow$ Send to Repeater (`Ctrl+R`).
Switch to the Repeater tab.
Insert test payloads into input fields (e.g., single quotes `'` or SQL strings like `' OR '1'='1`).
Click Send and inspect status codes, response times, and returned body text.
🔎 Investigation
Inspect Hidden Input Fields & Cookies: Examine raw request headers and body parameters for unencrypted sensitive values or improper authorization tokens.
Identify Broken Access Control: Compare response outputs when swapping session tokens between higher-privileged and lower-privileged user accounts.
Check Security Headers: Verify whether responses include modern defense headers such as `Content-Security-Policy`, `X-Frame-Options`, and `Strict-Transport-Security`.
⚠️ Important
HTTPS Interception: Intercepting encrypted HTTPS traffic requires installing Burp Suite’s CA Certificate into the client browser.
Intruder Rate Limits: The free (Community Edition) version throttles Intruder attack speeds significantly compared to the Professional version.
Authorization: Intercepting or testing web applications without explicit written permission violates authorization policies.
🛡️ Security
Sanitize and validate all client-side inputs on the server side; never rely solely on browser-level validation or hidden form fields.
Implement robust authentication controls, including Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) and rate limiting against automated login attempts.
